index 9167187..3dad24f 100644
--- a/docs/partners.md
+++ b/docs/partners.md
@@ -3,3 +3,13 @@
 Acme Logistics receives `order.created` webhooks and validates every payload against
 `schemas/webhook_order_v1.json` (strict: unknown fields are rejected and the delivery is
 dropped). Any new field needs a v2 schema and 30 days' notice to Acme.
+
+`order_created_payload(order)` continues to emit the exact v1 fields for CNY orders.
+EUR orders require `order_created_payload(order, version=2)` and a partner that has
+opted into `schemas/webhook_order_v2.json`; requesting v1 for EUR raises `ValueError`
+so the amount cannot be mistaken for CNY. Both versions express amounts in the
+currency's minor unit (fen for CNY, cents for EUR).
+
+Before enabling EUR delivery to Acme, provide the v2 schema, give the required
+30 days' notice, and confirm their opt-in. The caller must select the partner's
+negotiated version; there is no automatic version upgrade or delivery routing here.
diff --git a/docs/runbook.md b/docs/runbook.md
index ac9990b..bac37e1 100644
--- a/docs/runbook.md
+++ b/docs/runbook.md
@@ -5,3 +5,41 @@
 - Adding a column with a NOT NULL default rewrites the whole table on Postgres 10 and
   locks it for the duration (last time: 47 minutes of failed checkouts). Add the column
   nullable, backfill in batches of 10k, then add the constraint.
+
+## Currency rollout
+
+1. Apply `migrations/0007_orders_currency.sql` before deploying code that writes
+   currency. It adds a nullable column, then sets the CNY default separately (no
+   rewrite), preserving old writers. The support check permits NULL during backfill.
+   DDL still needs a brief exclusive lock; its 5-second lock timeout aborts the
+   transaction if busy, so retry during a quieter period.
+2. Backfill historical NULLs as CNY, committing each batch separately. For integer
+   order IDs, process consecutive ID ranges at most 10,000 wide from the minimum
+   through the maximum historical ID. For each range run the following in its own
+   transaction with bound `start_id` and `end_id` parameters, persisting the next
+   range after commit. Never overwrite an explicit currency:
+
+   ```sql
+   UPDATE orders SET currency = 'CNY'
+   WHERE id >= :start_id AND id < :end_id AND currency IS NULL;
+   ```
+
+   Pace batches against checkout latency and replica lag. Repeat the NULL check
+   after all writers have been upgraded; explicit NULL writes must have stopped.
+3. Validate separately: `ALTER TABLE orders VALIDATE CONSTRAINT orders_currency_supported;`
+   Confirm `SELECT count(*) FROM orders WHERE currency IS NULL;` returns zero.
+   In a scheduled maintenance window, finalize with
+   `ALTER TABLE orders ALTER COLUMN currency SET NOT NULL;` using a short lock
+   timeout. On PostgreSQL 10, SET NOT NULL scans the table under an exclusive lock;
+   measure this on staging first and defer finalization if that outage is unacceptable.
+   The initial migration intentionally leaves the column nullable.
+4. Deploy all workers with cache schema 4; old cache entries are ignored. Retire v3
+   entries after all old workers stop (the included in-memory cache has no TTL).
+   A rolling deployment requires EUR intake to wait until all workers understand currency.
+5. Move mixed-currency dashboard inputs to `daily_revenue_by_currency`. The legacy
+   `daily_revenue` returns an integer in CNY by default and rejects mismatched
+   currencies; pass `currency="EUR"` for an EUR-only input. No FX conversion is done.
+6. Enable EUR intake only after consumers support currency and partner v2 notice /
+   opt-in requirements in `docs/partners.md` are complete. Do not roll back to code
+   that ignores currency after EUR orders exist; disable EUR intake first and keep
+   currency-aware readers. Retain the column and its data on rollback.
